+# UI sounds: playing on time
+
+## The problem
+
+`UiSounds.play` created a new `Sound` for every cue and destroyed it when it
+ended. Once the last Sound using an asset is gone, the engine can drop that
+asset. The next cue then has to load it again before it can play. That is why
+`IsLoaded` was `false` when a cue started and the click came 0.3 to 0.5 s late,
+worst on phones with slower storage and network. `ContentProvider:PreloadAsync`
+at startup did not help: it loads the asset once, but nothing kept a Sound
+referencing it afterward, so later cues loaded it again.
+
+## What changed
+
+- **`UiSounds.luau`** now has a counted cache:
+  - `UiSounds.hold(soundId)` keeps one cached `Sound` per id in
+    `SoundService.UiSoundCache` and returns a release function. Several holders
+    of the same id share one Sound. The last release destroys it (deferred by a
+    frame, so a quick release and re-hold keeps it).
+  - `UiSounds.play(soundId)` clones the cached Sound, so the asset is already
+    loaded and the cue starts at once. If nothing holds the id, it falls back to
+    the old behavior (plays late) and warns once per id in Studio, so a missing
+    hold shows up in Output.
+  - `UiSounds.isCached(soundId)` is there for specs and in-game checks.
+  - `hold` does nothing when the game is not running, so stories and edit-mode
+    code cache nothing.
+- **`Main.client.luau`**: the HUD (`app`) holds `Click` and `PickerOpen` for its
+  whole life. `Click` is the shared button sound that later screens will play
+  too. `PickerOpen` plays the moment build mode starts, and the Build button or
+  the B key can start it at any time, so it must be loaded before then. The
+  `PreloadAsync` call is removed; the holds replace it.
+- **`BlockPicker.luau`**: the picker holds `PickerClose` while it is open, so the
+  close sound is loaded only while build mode is on and is freed after it
+  closes.
+
+Adding a screen: hold the sounds only that screen plays from its component body,
+with `cleanup(UiSounds.hold(SoundIds.X))`, so they are cached while it is open.
+Do not hold `Click` again; the HUD already does.
+
+One remaining edge: `PickerClose` starts loading when the picker opens, so
+closing it again within the load time (about 0.4 s on a slow phone, the very
+first time) can still play that close sound late.
+
+## Checking it in Studio
+
+1. Start a playtest (on a phone emulator profile too, if you want the worst
+   case). Output should show no "played without a hold" warning while you use
+   the Build button and the picker.
+2. In the Explorer, open `SoundService > UiSoundCache`. With build mode off it
+   holds two Sounds (Click and PickerOpen). Turn build mode on: a third
+   (PickerClose) appears. Turn it off: it is gone a frame later.
+3. To check the timing directly, run this in the client command bar while
+   playing, then click Build and the tiles:
+
+   ```lua
+   game:GetService("SoundService").ChildAdded:Connect(function(child)
+   	if child:IsA("Sound") then
+   		task.defer(function()
+   			print(child.SoundId, "IsLoaded:", child.IsLoaded)
+   		end)
+   	end
+   end)
+   ```
+
+   Each cue should print `IsLoaded: true`. Before the change, they printed
+   `false`. Step 2 is the way to see what is cached: the command bar gets its
+   own copy of any module it requires, so `UiSounds.isCached` read from there
+   always reports nothing cached.
